#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050522 :topic/id #uuid "503f6e39-70fd-54b8-a04b-77e9fcd7b979" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152291 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151984 :question/id #uuid "bbf72227-f0da-5dab-88cd-bfed2ed773f7" :question/math-academy-id "e-6792" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $y'' - 3y'+ 2y=0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $y_1(x) = e^{x}$ is a solution to the equation.\n\nII. $y_2(x) = e^{2x}$ is a solution to the equation.\n\nIII. The general solution of the equation is $y=Ae^{x} + Be^{2x}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's check each statement.\n\n- To determine whether $y_1(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute $y_1$ and its derivatives into the equation. Computing the first and second derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & ={e}^{x} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{x},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting into the equation, we get\n\n$$\ne^{x}  - 3e^{x} + 2e^{x} = 0. \\color{green}\\quad\\checkmark\n$$\n\nSo $y_1$ is a solution to the equation, and therefore statement I is true.\n\n- Similarly, we compute the derivatives of $y_2$ and substitute to the equation. Computing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =2{e}^{2x} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =4{e}^{2x},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting into the equation, we get\n\n$$\n4e^{2x}  - 3(2e^{2x})  + 2(e^{2x} ) = 0. \\quad\\color{green}\\checkmark\n$$\n\nSo $y_2$ is a solution to the equation, and therefore statement II is true.\n\n- Both $y_1=e^x$ and $y_2=e^{2x}$ are solutions of the equation. So, the general solution is $y=Ae^{x} + Be^{2x}$ if $y_1$ and $y_2$ are linearly independent. To check if they are linearly independent, we compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}{({e}^{2x})}^{′}-{({e}^{x})}^{′}{e}^{2x} \\\\  & =2{e}^{3x}-{e}^{3x} \\\\  & ={e}^{3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian does not equal $0,$ the functions are linearly independent. Therefore, statement III is true.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct statements are I, II, and III."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6302f81d-4834-56a5-b014-0a97c3dccd6f" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152635 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9749d3be-4c56-5215-88d8-f44e2216a000" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150318} {:answer/id #uuid "bf633b75-b62b-5af5-8192-a7d77da32b52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150319} {:answer/id #uuid "3cf0d8bb-97ce-5100-a3ab-54f44d605bcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150320} {:answer/id #uuid "3f6d4bbe-2bcd-5614-9d8f-507597f0556e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150321} {:answer/id #uuid "7cd43998-b590-57f1-b8de-33fda2826f4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150322}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9749d3be-4c56-5215-88d8-f44e2216a000" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150318} :answer-field/id #uuid "c352168e-972c-545d-8ea6-1c1dba22e461" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150317}] :question/id #uuid "77507b82-4237-560c-91e2-186512253118" :question/math-academy-id "q-65095" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $y^{″} - y^{′} - 2y = 0$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $y_{1}(x) = e^{2x}$ is a solution to the equation.\nII. $y_{2}(x) = e^{2x + 3}$ is a solution to the equation.\n3. The general solution of the equation is $y = Ae^{2x} + Be^{2x + 3}$." :question/worked-solution "Both $e^{2x}$ and $e^{2x+3}=e^3e^{2x}$ satisfy $y''-y'-2y=0$. However, they are constant multiples of one another, so $Ae^{2x}+Be^{2x+3}=(A+Be^3)e^{2x}$ contains only one independent homogeneous solution. The characteristic equation $(r-2)(r+1)=0$ also gives $e^{-x}$, and the general solution is $C_1e^{2x}+C_2e^{-x}$. Therefore I and II are true, and III is false."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152643 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b1f6bbb0-8bb4-5814-8291-77d781e91d7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150366} {:answer/id #uuid "eb334d08-6cf4-5607-837b-858814841a27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150367} {:answer/id #uuid "f49738bc-c5dc-5ebf-b55a-56de03bbe591" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150368} {:answer/id #uuid "2549001f-aa99-5aff-98b6-a6d33d7a120d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150369} {:answer/id #uuid "76c96a0f-47ba-5a43-ba60-7937000fad2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150370}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f49738bc-c5dc-5ebf-b55a-56de03bbe591" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150368} :answer-field/id #uuid "44ef2de9-a443-55fe-a400-2d9f9ca946c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150365}] :question/id #uuid "e160d551-48fe-5a54-97de-ae052256db02" :question/math-academy-id "q-65175" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $x^{2}y^{″} - 2xy^{′} + 2y = 0$. Work on an interval where $x\\ne0$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $y_{1}(x) = x$ is a solution to the equation.\nII. $y_{2}(x) = x^{2}$ is a solution to the equation.\n3. The general solution of the equation is $y = Ax + Bx^{2}$." :question/worked-solution "On an interval where $x\\ne0$, direct substitution shows that $x$ and $x^2$ both satisfy $x^2y''-2xy'+2y=0$. Their Wronskian is $x(2x)-x^2=x^2\\ne0$ on that interval. They form a fundamental set, so the general solution is $y=Ax+Bx^2$. All three statements are true."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265795 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b0b037aa-3626-50b8-855c-2b40fcdd1169" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463820} {:answer/id #uuid "5df012f3-927e-50e5-b24a-e1883265a1fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463821}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5df012f3-927e-50e5-b24a-e1883265a1fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463821} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd27258e-ef1a-559d-990a-8e41a30b2d8b" :answer-field/key "${y}_{1}(x)=x$ is a solution to the equation" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464844} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b51bd1c8-800b-51d7-af75-17509a5e46ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463822} {:answer/id #uuid "4a4dd6b2-1594-57b1-a1df-762b74203c71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463823}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4a4dd6b2-1594-57b1-a1df-762b74203c71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463823} :answer-field/id #uuid "3022ceba-00e2-5b11-877e-c5caeb1e94de" :answer-field/key "${y}_{2}(x)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is a solution to the equation" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464845} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1084f180-252c-5ffa-8ee1-e7cd97927caf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463824} {:answer/id #uuid "de93ff4a-6849-5911-b001-13d857922885" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463825}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de93ff4a-6849-5911-b001-13d857922885" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463825} :answer-field/id #uuid "13765578-b450-5f75-b50d-6e1db6510f05" :answer-field/key "The general solution of the equation is $y=Ax+Bx\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464846} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fa6539ac-efb4-5a39-9e7b-99db2b98a55f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186667946} {:answer/id #uuid "58749d48-cd9d-56df-8ed5-05d89c5bfa58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186667947}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fa6539ac-efb4-5a39-9e7b-99db2b98a55f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186667946} :answer-field/id #uuid "25db7d0c-02a4-5ece-95c0-18154bf776e1" :answer-field/key "None of the above" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668250}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a94ff3b3-b6c6-5496-9eb9-eaf4ceef3036" :question/math-academy-id "q-130003" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}-x{y}^{′}+y=0.$ For $x>0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's check each statement:\n\n- To determine whether ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute ${y}_{1}$ and its derivatives into the equation.\n\nFirst, we find the first and second derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =1 \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation. We get\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}(0)-x(1)+x=0.\\,✓\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}$ is a solution to the equation, and therefore statement “${y}_{1}(x)=x$ is a solution to the equation” is true.\n\n- Similarly, we compute the derivatives of ${y}_{2}$ and substitute to the equation. Computing the derivatives yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation. We get\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)-x(1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x=0.\\,✓\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}$ is a solution to the equation, and therefore statement “${y}_{2}(x)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is a solution to the equation” is true.\n\n- So, both ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the equation. To check if these functions are linearly independent, we compute the Wronskian.\n\nThe Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of two functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix}={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =(x)(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{′}-(x)^{′}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\  & =x(1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-(1)x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\  & =x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the  Wronskian is not equal to $0,$ the functions are linearly independent, hence $y=Ax+Bx\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$  is the general solution.\n\nTherefore, statement “The general solution of the equation is $y=Ax+Bx\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$” is true."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345315 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e581553-7210-5abb-9931-ac7dcea3e1c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451663} {:answer/id #uuid "55076e83-c78e-5284-9f2b-c4a27717b35c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451664}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "55076e83-c78e-5284-9f2b-c4a27717b35c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451664} :answer-field/id #uuid "bdb68a00-b35c-59a8-9c34-a815bc238d27" :answer-field/key "${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x$ is a solution to the equation." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452679} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b48259f-12b5-5dab-a297-c2056caa4a27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451665} {:answer/id #uuid "715ff4d5-270c-5249-87e8-98885ca991d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451666}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b48259f-12b5-5dab-a297-c2056caa4a27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451665} :answer-field/id #uuid "d3c6b10e-16d0-55db-ab4f-6f3e039de65a" :answer-field/key "${y}_{2}(x)={\\sin }^{2}⁡x$ is a solution to the equation." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452680} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dbed77a4-db9e-5253-974d-145db4aab206" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451667} {:answer/id #uuid "34881740-3e21-5aba-8c87-22dbdd46ec2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451668}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dbed77a4-db9e-5253-974d-145db4aab206" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451667} :answer-field/id #uuid "02984b7a-3e7b-534d-8216-479fd4d5365d" :answer-field/key "The general solution of the equation is $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+B{\\sin }^{2}⁡x.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452681}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b3fcfe57-83d0-5b89-abde-7c89002de38a" :question/math-academy-id "q-77674" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation ${y}^{″}+4y=0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's check each statement:\n\n- To determine whether ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute ${y}_{1}$ and its derivatives into the equation.\n\nFirst, we find the first and second derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & =-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation. We get\n\n$$\n-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)=0.\\,✓\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}$ is a solution to the equation, and therefore statement “${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x$ is a solution to the equation” is true.\n\n- Similarly, we compute the derivatives of ${y}_{2}$ and substitute to the equation. Computing the derivatives yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =-2{\\sin }^{2}⁡x+2{\\cos }^{2}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation. We get\n\n$$\n-2{\\sin }^{2}⁡x+2{\\cos }^{2}⁡x+4({\\sin }^{2}⁡x)=2{\\sin }^{2}⁡x+2{\\cos }^{2}⁡x=2.\\,\\times \n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}$ is not a solution to the equation, and therefore statement “${y}_{2}(x)={\\sin }^{2}⁡x$ is a solution to the equation” is false.\n\n- So, ${y}_{1}$ is a solution while ${y}_{2}$ is not a solution of the equation, hence $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+B{\\sin }^{2}⁡x$  is not the general solution.\n\nTherefore, statement “The general solution of the equation is $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+B{\\sin }^{2}⁡x.$” is false."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348312 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f7570e3d-575a-53ae-b8a8-f38567dd5f67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451759} {:answer/id #uuid "6a94344d-679e-53eb-a314-dbd682508d6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451760}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6a94344d-679e-53eb-a314-dbd682508d6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451760} :answer-field/id #uuid "6cac5207-2e26-5568-942f-86862d63daeb" :answer-field/key "${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{-x}$ is a solution to the equation." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452727} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a7f0c9f7-0402-5153-a0b1-122f69f189bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451761} {:answer/id #uuid "87688b5e-8c4f-5df9-8fe4-74a28e2626b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451762}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "87688b5e-8c4f-5df9-8fe4-74a28e2626b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451762} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ab8e285-5d80-5832-a834-3288e7c3d6ed" :answer-field/key "${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{1-x}$ is a solution to the equation." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452728} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7bba18c2-f91a-5e2e-ba0a-2d83da494501" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451763} {:answer/id #uuid "c0839935-7605-5c99-afdd-d828e290b557" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451764}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7bba18c2-f91a-5e2e-ba0a-2d83da494501" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451763} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ec233c5-5e09-59b8-9599-d16f1338842d" :answer-field/key "The general solution of the equation is $y=A{e}^{-x}+B{e}^{1-x}.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452729}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "435df6d1-711b-52a7-a1be-e3012765e1bb" :question/math-academy-id "q-79930" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation ${y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+y=0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's check each statement:\n\n- To determine whether ${y}_{1}(t)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute ${y}_{1}$ and its derivatives into the equation.\n\nFirst, we find the first and second derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(t) & =-{e}^{-x} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-x}+2(-{e}^{-x})+{e}^{-x} & =(1-2+1){e}^{-x}=0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}$ is a solution to the equation, and therefore statement “${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{-x}$ is a solution to the equation” is true.\n\n- Similarly, we compute the derivatives of ${y}_{2}$ and substitute to the equation. Computing the derivatives yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(t) & =-{e}^{1-x} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{1-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{1-x}+2(-{e}^{1-x})+{e}^{1-x} & =(1-2+1){e}^{1-x}=0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}$ is a solution to the equation, and therefore statement “${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{1-x}$ is a solution to the equation” is true.\n\n- So, both ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the equation. To check if these functions are linearly independent, we compute the Wronskian.\n\nThe Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of two functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix}={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & ={e}^{-x}{({e}^{1-x})}^{′}-{({e}^{-x})}^{′}{e}^{1-x} \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}(-{e}^{1-x})-(-{e}^{-x}){e}^{1-x} \\\\  & =-{e}^{1-2t}+{e}^{1-2t} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the  Wronskian equals $0,$ the functions are not linearly independent, hence $y=A{e}^{-x}+B{e}^{1-x}$  is not the general solution.\n\nTherefore, statement “The general solution of the equation is $y=A{e}^{-x}+B{e}^{1-x}.$” is false."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357466 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eb85e8eb-30f1-5e30-88c6-2582220a10a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451893} {:answer/id #uuid "951c19d3-6d68-50a9-9a17-2c166c688f6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451894}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "951c19d3-6d68-50a9-9a17-2c166c688f6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451894} :answer-field/id #uuid "db880168-c319-51c9-8b78-70142c95d5ec" :answer-field/key "${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x$ is a solution to the equation." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452794} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b9432fd1-b127-528c-9c36-1845ecfb49e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451895} {:answer/id #uuid "a669c147-cbc3-57f4-b038-ac51d2ff09fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451896}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a669c147-cbc3-57f4-b038-ac51d2ff09fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451896} :answer-field/id #uuid "050c8270-4795-5516-8572-0dd11186bc74" :answer-field/key "${y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x$ is a solution to the equation." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452795} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b9611986-1e99-5e63-bd2e-d8b8e11bc95d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451897} {:answer/id #uuid "5642b8fb-b64d-532e-a7f5-75734f821b5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451898}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5642b8fb-b64d-532e-a7f5-75734f821b5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451898} :answer-field/id #uuid "900c0ac8-9b40-5d86-815d-5a4462740f32" :answer-field/key "The general solution of the equation is $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452796}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "bd483d10-bcb0-5d3a-aec6-2a397c70d709" :question/math-academy-id "q-90699" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation ${y}^{″}+16y=0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's check each statement:\n\n- To determine whether ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute ${y}_{1}$ and its derivatives into the equation.\n\nFirst, we find the first and second derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & =-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation. We get\n\n$$\n-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+16(\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x)=0.\\,✓\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}$ is a solution to the equation, and therefore statement “${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x$ is a solution to the equation” is true.\n\n- Similarly, we compute the derivatives of ${y}_{2}$ and substitute to the equation. Computing the derivatives yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =-16\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation. We get\n\n$$\n-16\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+16(\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x)=0.\\,✓\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}$ is a solution to the equation, and therefore statement “${y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x$ is a solution to the equation” is true.\n\n- So, both ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the equation. To check if these functions are linearly independent, we compute the Wronskian.\n\nThe Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of two functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix}={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x)}^{′}-{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x)}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x(\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x)-(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x \\\\  & =4({\\cos }^{2}⁡4x+{\\sin }^{2}⁡4x) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the  Wronskian is not equal to $0,$ the functions are linearly independent, hence $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x$  is the general solution.\n\nTherefore, statement “The general solution of the equation is $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x.$” is true."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400443 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "84c8f04c-de6b-5dce-8146-3527e7f3c7e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472628} {:answer/id #uuid "0061914e-dc5a-5b4b-b20d-0dff9b1f199a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472629}] :answer-field/correct {:an