ld/key "The general solution of the equation is $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}^{2}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473711} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56123df6-ed65-5c2a-909b-9ef4fadd4657" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186669031} {:answer/id #uuid "e0363747-31e8-529c-b26c-8b3415898324" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186669032}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "56123df6-ed65-5c2a-909b-9ef4fadd4657" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186669031} :answer-field/id #uuid "2febe8fa-ecdc-5023-bf94-ab246b66e148" :answer-field/key "None of the above" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186669315}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5007768b-5de4-5206-bbd7-b32aa44bd388" :question/math-academy-id "q-130005" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $x{y}^{″}-{y}^{′}+4{x}^{3}y=0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's check each statement:\n\n- To determine whether ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute ${y}_{1}$ and its derivatives into the equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & =-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation. We get\n\n$$\nx(-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)-(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)+4{x}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x=(4{x}^{3}-4x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\\,\\times \n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}$ is not a solution to the equation, and therefore statement “${y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}^{2}$ is a solution to the equation” is false.\n\n- Similarly, we compute the derivatives of ${y}_{2}$ and substitute to the equation. Computing the derivatives yields\n\nFirst, we find the first and second derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡{x}^{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡{x}^{2}-4{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation. We get\n\n$$\nx(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡{x}^{2}-4{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}^{2})-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡{x}^{2}+4{x}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}^{2}=(2x-2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡{x}^{2}+(4{x}^{3}-4{x}^{3})\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}^{2}=0.\\,✓\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}$ is a solution to the equation, and therefore statement “${y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}^{2}$ is a solution to the equation” is true.\n\n- So, ${y}_{2}$ is a solution while ${y}_{1}$ is not a solution of the equation, hence $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}^{2}$  is not the general solution.\n\nTherefore, statement “The general solution of the equation is $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}^{2}$” is false."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421820 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4049eacb-f333-5928-be15-ad4db6bd4e7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474308} {:answer/id #uuid "35601916-369e-51fe-abf4-791c123c637e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474309}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "35601916-369e-51fe-abf4-791c123c637e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474309} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b95782b-8b17-5f77-9801-8a99d11ccf2a" :answer-field/key "${y}_{1}(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$ is a solution to the equation" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475080} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b8d5ec7-084e-5c9f-8f80-d0e39dff32c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474310} {:answer/id #uuid "a3e17bc7-fee0-5214-bbe1-e420ad24d8df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474311}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b8d5ec7-084e-5c9f-8f80-d0e39dff32c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474310} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a78c060-c106-55f9-a38d-38117b0a316f" :answer-field/key "${y}_{2}(x)={x}^{4}$ is a solution to the equation" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475081} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45cf437a-d3e6-5bff-a122-cc094f36db9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474312} {:answer/id #uuid "91b92ce5-41cb-5b8f-8a7e-42023c7326d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474313}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "45cf437a-d3e6-5bff-a122-cc094f36db9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474312} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc403eaf-5b3a-598a-b3ae-28f5e22f4037" :answer-field/key "The general solution of the equation is $y=\\frac{A}{{x}^{2}}+B{x}^{4}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475082} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "70e7263c-5b08-5b3d-9dec-3122e3a76147" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186669029} {:answer/id #uuid "1edc2164-4af9-50e7-bd0a-c9e35b34ec64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186669030}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "70e7263c-5b08-5b3d-9dec-3122e3a76147" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186669029} :answer-field/id #uuid "cdced459-bca1-5376-ad85-fcf1b5f948d1" :answer-field/key "None of the above" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186669314}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "70cf3885-2e2c-5971-b49c-1988c3e0bb2b" :question/math-academy-id "q-130004" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}+2x{y}^{′}-2y=0,$ where $x>0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's check each statement:\n\n- To determine whether ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute ${y}_{1}$ and its derivatives into the equation.\n\nFirst, we find the first and second derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{6}{{x}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\left(\\frac{6}{{x}^{4}}\\right)+2x\\left(-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}\\right)-2\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\right) & =\\frac{6}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}=0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}$ is a solution to the equation, and therefore statement “${y}_{1}(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$ is a solution to the equation” is true.\n\n- Similarly, we compute the derivatives of ${y}_{2}$ and substitute to the equation. Computing the derivatives yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =4{x}^{3} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =12{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation. We get\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}(12{x}^{2})+2x(4{x}^{3})-2{x}^{4}=18{x}^{4}.\\,\\times \n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}$ is not a solution to the equation, and therefore statement “${y}_{2}(x)={x}^{4}$ is a solution to the equation” is false.\n\n- So, ${y}_{1}$ is a solution but ${y}_{2}$ is not a solution of the equation, hence $y=\\frac{A}{{x}^{2}}+B{x}^{4}$ is not the general solution.\n\nTherefore, statement “The general solution of the equation is $y=\\frac{A}{{x}^{2}}+B{x}^{4}$” is false."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421821 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4025a2da-8220-50a7-b4f3-219e6d29cc87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474302} {:answer/id #uuid "91c226a9-4c73-5b94-92b0-1a733df3a834" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474303}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4025a2da-8220-50a7-b4f3-219e6d29cc87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474302} :answer-field/id #uuid "f318ba66-ccb9-5f8e-9231-0f5fb32052aa" :answer-field/key "${y}_{1}(x)=x{e}^{x}$ is a solution to the equation" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475077} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eb83e9ac-cb3a-5dc7-8842-0118c6b9c9ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474304} {:answer/id #uuid "74a023e8-6ba2-5b3f-a47a-189a8481e140" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474305}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "74a023e8-6ba2-5b3f-a47a-189a8481e140" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474305} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf5c45ac-f6f4-5fdf-87b6-c55e5d17a5ea" :answer-field/key "${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{2x}$ is a solution to the equation" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475078} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab0e9691-308c-596d-9c85-fd9d5dd60a50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474306} {:answer/id #uuid "5c2a6d79-cf64-517d-a658-e230018451ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474307}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ab0e9691-308c-596d-9c85-fd9d5dd60a50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474306} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a04d4f0-4217-55ed-ab79-e4478a4fd20e" :answer-field/key "The general solution of the equation is $y=Ax{e}^{x}+B{e}^{2x}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475079} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d74bd09b-264f-5634-8054-3f1c70f9d9bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186669027} {:answer/id #uuid "f6925548-e106-5d17-ba52-3285a65522e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186669028}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d74bd09b-264f-5634-8054-3f1c70f9d9bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186669027} :answer-field/id #uuid "08494236-2896-5a39-9689-fad6beba874e" :answer-field/key "None of the above" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186669313}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7fd65812-f6ae-584d-bfe8-d11797aed930" :question/math-academy-id "q-130000" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation ${y}^{″}-4y=0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's check each statement:\n\n- To determine whether ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute ${y}_{1}$ and its derivatives into the equation.\n\nFirst, we find the first and second derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =(1+x){e}^{x} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & =(2+x){e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation. We get\n\n$$\n(2+x){e}^{x}-4(x{e}^{x})=(2-3x){e}^{x}.\\,\\times \n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}$ is not a solution to the equation, and therefore statement “${y}_{1}(x)=x{e}^{x}$ is a solution to the equation” is false.\n\n- Similarly, we compute the derivatives of ${y}_{2}$ and substitute to the equation. Computing the derivatives yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =2{e}^{2x} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =4{e}^{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation. We get\n\n$$\n4{e}^{2x}-4{e}^{2x}=0.\\,✓\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}$ is a solution to the equation, and therefore statement “${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{2x}$ is a solution to the equation” is true.\n\n- So, ${y}_{1}$ is not a solution while ${y}_{2}$ is a solution of the equation, hence $y=Ax{e}^{x}+B{e}^{2x}$ is not the general solution.\n\nTherefore, statement “The general solution of the equation is $y=Ax{e}^{x}+B{e}^{2x}$” is false."}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About General Solutions of Second-Order Linear ODEs"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152292 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151982 :question/id #uuid "bacb284c-88db-5ad5-bd7d-cbe6fbc604df" :question/math-academy-id "e-6789" :question/problem "Given that $y_1(x) = e^{5x}$ is a solution to the equation $y'' - 10y' + 25y = 0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution $y_2(x)$ to the equation, and write down the general solution." :question/worked-solution "According to the reduction of order method, we let $y_{2}=u(x) e^{5x}.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{e}^{5x}+5u{e}^{5x}={e}^{5x}({u}^{′}+5u) \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={e}^{5x}({u}^{″}+10{u}^{′}+25u).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{5x}({u}^{″}+10{u}^{′}+25u)-10{e}^{5x}({u}^{′}+5u)+25{e}^{5x}u & =0 \\\\ {e}^{5x}({u}^{″}+10{u}^{′}+25u-10{u}^{′}-50u+25u) & =0 \\\\ {e}^{5x}({u}^{″}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $e^{5x}\\neq 0$ we must have\n\n$$\nu'' = 0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating the equation twice gives\n\n$$\nu(x) = c_1x+c_2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for $y_2(x) = ue^{5x}$ becomes\n\n$$\ny_2(x) = e^{5x}\\left(c_1 x + c_2\\right).\n$$\n\nSetting $c_1 = 1$ and $c_2 = 0,$ we get\n\n$$\ny_2(x) = e^{5x}\\cdot x = xe^{5x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W(y_1, y_2) = e^{10x} \\neq 0,$ we know that $y_1$ and $y_2$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the equation is\n\n$$\ny=Ae^{5x}+Bxe^{5x}.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a67ca0bc-c946-5963-a733-2ec9eb6b07ce" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152640 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b809e817-7641-5aa4-88a9-cc634e1300cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = A\\cos 5x + B\\sin 5x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150348} {:answer/id #uuid "e29816cd-782a-5491-97c1-5a376da85d27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = A\\cos 5x + B\\sec 5x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150349} {:answer/id #uuid "1c3d39df-ac25-50e0-8510-c82fa0edba9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = A\\cos 5x + Bx\\cos 5x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150350} {:answer/id #uuid "2018e5fe-0186-5531-98dd-c234dc26e300" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = A\\cos 5x + B" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150351} {:answer/id #uuid "33f2cf02-3de1-5d67-8012-ba2bbdb4e0a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = A\\cos 5x + B\\tan 5x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150352}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b809e817-7641-5aa4-88a9-cc634e1300cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = A\\cos 5x + B\\sin 5x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150348} :answer-field/id #uuid "d3c306cb-0a41-5fa7-8718-1b3f40fd9009" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150347}] :question/id #uuid "517a2321-90b3-58d8-9be2-9305fdb17bd2" :question/math-academy-id "q-65118" :question/problem "Given that $y_{1}(x) = \\cos 5x$ is a solution to the equation $y^{″} + 25y = 0$, use reduction of order to find a second solution $y_{2}(x)$ to the equation, and write down the general solution." :question/worked-solution "Set $y_2=u(x)\\cos5x$ on an interval where $\\cos5x\\ne0$. Differentiation and substitution give\n$$\ny_2''+25y_2=u''\\cos5x-10u'\\sin5x=0.\n$$\nLetting $w=u'$ yields $w'=10w\\tan5x$, so $w=C\\sec^2 5x$ and $u=\\frac C5\\tan5x+D$. Choosing $C=5,D=0$ gives $y_2=\\sin5x$, which extends across the zeros of $\\cos5x$ and solves the original equation on $\\mathbb R$. Since $W(\\cos5x,\\sin5x)=5\\ne0$, the general solution is\n$$\ny=A\\cos5x+B\\sin5x.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152641 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f3cfcca-72ae-5e71-b6f4-b3f5b4b6f072" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = Ae^{4t} + Be^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150354} {:answer/id #uuid "eefe210c-2485-540e-ab37-32449c82d52b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = Ae^{4t} + Be^{-3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150355} {:answer/id #uuid "0b7e8228-c507-5ec6-bd91-475e11831da9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = Ae^{4t} + Bt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150356} {:answer/id #uuid "24749364-6e97-5fc5-906e-1a5b12621f4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = Ae^{4t} + Be^{-t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150357} {:answer/id #uuid "39c4f4cc-1cb9-55df-bef3-30a7ed5fefb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = Ae^{4t} + Bte^{4t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150358}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eefe210c-2485-540e-ab37-32449c82d52b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = Ae^{4t} + Be^{-3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150355} :answer-field/id #uuid "95911cc3-28c1-57dc-a313-c6fca4dd3a3c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150353}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "13f41b02-c7b6-5e8f-b658-ad759f61ba18" :question/math-academy-id "q-65119" :question/problem "Given that $y_{1}(t) = e^{4t}$ is a solution to the equation $y^{″} - y^{′} - 12y = 0$, use reduction of order to find a second solution $y_{2}(t)$ to the equation, and write down the general solution." :question/worked-solution "Set $y_2=u(t)e^{4t}$. Then\n$$\ny_2'=e^{4t}(u'+4u),\\qquad y_2''=e^{4t}(u''+8u'+16u).\n$$\nSubstitution into $y''-y'-12y=0$ gives $e^{4t}(u''+7u')=0$. Since $e^{4t}\\ne0$, let $w=u'$ and solve $w'+7w=0$: $w=Ce^{-7t}$ and $u=-\\frac C7e^{-7t}+D$. Taking $C=-7,D=0$ gives $y_2=e^{-3t}$. The Wronskian is $-7e^t\\ne0$, so\n$$\ny=Ae^{4t}+Be^{-3t}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265788 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f1c568d-36bc-51c0-84f0-502d1edec67c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263134} {:answer/id #uuid "15c6b34c-c545-5d3c-acb9-3150c86a0408" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263204} {:answer/id #uuid "6991653b-499f-5aa9-839d-82c296e784eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{x}+Bx{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263907} {:answer/id #uuid "a4b70ef3-acdc-5304-9dab-af64de3228a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264404} {:answer/id #uuid "f636dd7a-29d2-590e-895b-f51e6bc5bb9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265154}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6991653b-499f-5aa9-839d-82c296e784eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{x}+Bx{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263907} :answer-field/id #uuid "becc03c6-febe-52bf-9a33-abc159de7662" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265577}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "edcd3710-0bbf-55f6-957d-428926b6e84b" :question/math-academy-id "q-129942" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x}$ is a solution to the equation ${y}^{″}-2{y}^{′}+y=0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(x)$ to the equation, and write down the general solution." :question/worked-solution "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(x){e}^{x}.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{e}^{x}+u{e}^{x} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{e}^{x}+2{u}^{′}{e}^{x}+u{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({u}^{″}{e}^{x}+2{u}^{′}{e}^{x}+u{e}^{x})-2({u}^{′}{e}^{x}+u{e}^{x})+u{e}^{x} & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}{e}^{x}+2{u}^{′}{e}^{x}+u{e}^{x}-2{u}^{′}{e}^{x}-2u{e}^{x}+u{e}^{x} & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}{e}^{x} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{x}\\ne 0$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n{w}^{′}=0.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw={c}_{1}.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}={c}_{1}\\,⟹\\,u={c}_{1}x+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(x)=u{e}^{x}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=({c}_{1}x+{c}_{2}){e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nSetting ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=x{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})={e}^{2x}\\ne 0,$ we know that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{x}+Bx{e}^{x}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265790 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cfa1112-cde9-5cf2-83d2-430f487290ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-2t}+B{e}^{-5t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263123} {:answer/id #uuid "123591e4-14bc-54fd-aea2-c5e4787430e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-2t}+Bt{e}^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263172} {:answer/id #uuid "904e81be-6f2f-541a-b7d0-8a35e1022f77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-2t}+B{e}^{-3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264238} {:answer/id #uuid "cbafd037-f483-5c44-a33a-d8f67e652aa9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-2t}+B{e}^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264785} {:answer/id #uuid "eb66a210-3c1e-577b-b412-6b9cb23b046a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-2t}+B{e}^{2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265053}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "904e81be-6f2f-541a-b7d0-8a35e1022f77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-2t}+B{e}^{-3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264238} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e28a020-0cab-5648-8aea-32cf23bc690b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265465}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a1408d84-fcf4-5ea6-946a-9cd3b9087562" :question/math-academy-id "q-129956" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(t)={e}^{-2t}$ is a solution to the equation ${y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}+6y=0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(t)$ to the equation, and write down the general solution." :question/worked-solution "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(t){e}^{-2t}.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{e}^{-2t}-2u{e}^{-2t} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{e}^{-2t}-4{u}^{′}{e}^{-2t}+4u{e}^{-2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({u}^{″}{e}^{-2t}-4{u}^{′}{e}^{-2t}+4u{e}^{-2t})+5({u}^{′}{e}^{-2t}-2u{e}^{-2t})+6u{e}^{-2t} & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}{e}^{-2t}-4{u}^{′}{e}^{-2t}+4u{e}^{-2t}+5{u}^{′}{e}^{-2t}-10u{e}^{-2t}+6u{e}^{-2t} & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}{e}^{-2t}+{u}^{′}{e}^{-2t} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{-2t}({u}^{″}+{u}^{′}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{-2t}\\ne 0$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{″}+{u}^{′}=0.\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n{w}^{′}+w=0.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw={c}_{1}{e}^{-t}.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}={c}_{1}{e}^{-t}\\,⟹\\,u=-{c}_{1}{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(t)=u{e}^{-2t}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(t)=(-{c}_{1}{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}){e}^{-2t}.\n$$\n\nSetting ${c}_{1}=-1$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-t}\\cdot {e}^{-2t}={e}^{-3t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=-{e}^{-5t}\\ne 0,$ we know that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-2t}+B{e}^{-3t}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265791 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3040c782-2737-5f57-8606-dffa49aa27aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{4t}+B{e}^{-4t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263409} {:answer/id #uuid "3ebb6fa1-15cf-59de-92be-9c94e0f2d102" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{4t}+Bt{e}^{4t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263553} {:answer/id #uuid "ae4120ad-d908-58da-b746-c57e32d1a603" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{4t}+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264497} {:answer/id #uuid "d5664e73-19c9-5f53-99ba-19827db53d5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{4t}+B{e}^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264859} {:answer/id #uuid "f646c609-0218-5a20-aedc-c6f293e54dce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{4t}+B{t}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265155}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3040c782-2737-5f57-8606-dffa49aa27aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{4t}+B{e}^{-4t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263409} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f50c074-c44b-5ac6-b498-96bd174204dc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265501}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "07185561-6548-5175-bde3-1e35630955ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-129957" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(t)={e}^{4t}$ is a solution to the equation ${y}^{″}-16y=0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(t)$ to the equation, and write down the general solution." :question/worked-solution "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(t){e}^{4t}.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{e}^{4t}+4u{e}^{4t} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{e}^{4t}+8{u}^{′}{e}^{4t}+16u{e}^{4t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({u}^{″}{e}^{4t}+8{u}^{′}{e}^{4t}+16u{e}^{4t})-16u{e}^{4t} & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}{e}^{4t}+8{u}^{′}{e}^{4t} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{4t}({u}^{″}+8{u}^{′}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{4t}\\ne 0$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{″}+8{u}^{′}=0.\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n{w}^{′}+8w=0.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw={c}_{1}{e}^{-8t}.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}={c}_{1}{e}^{-8t}\\,⟹\\,u=-\\frac{{c}_{1}}{8}{e}^{-8t}+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(t)=u{e}^{4t}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(t)=\\left(-\\frac{{c}_{1}}{8}{e}^{-8t}+{c}_{2}\\right){e}^{4t}.\n$$\n\nSetting ${c}_{1}=-8$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-8t}\\cdot {e}^{4t}={e}^{-4t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=-8\\ne 0,$ we know that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{4t}+B{e}^{-4t}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335797 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "135563a7-f82a-51f9-8b00-e8c145d3907a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{3t}+Bt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333160} {:answer/id #uuid "2c4d9197-9f6b-5b65-9d63-998021644d52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{3t}+B{e}^{2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333407} {:answer/id #uuid "7c71a082-77c3-5993-bfeb-12c89ae7b985" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{3t}+B{e}^{-3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334101} {:answer/id #uuid "8254c6c5-9276-53d1-9074-3a1df3423ff4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{3t}+Bt{e}^{3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334150} {:answer/id #uuid "9d41a5b4-e312-54dd-9ea9-4c5241af3d0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{3t}+B{e}^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334375}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8254c6c5-9276-53d1-9074-3a1df3423ff4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{3t}+Bt{e}^{3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334150} :answer-field/id #uuid "567975d3-e402-5426-a3fa-3993c8ec5326" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335412}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "66e836f0-6fcc-5ad0-b3e7-70c5200c7df5" :question/math-academy-id "q-65120" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(t)={e}^{3t}$ is a solution to the equation ${y}^{″}-6{y}^{′}+9y=0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(t)$ to the equation, and write down the general solution." :question/worked-solution "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(t){e}^{3t}.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{e}^{3t}+3u{e}^{3t} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{e}^{3t}+6{u}^{′}{e}^{3t}+9u{e}^{3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({u}^{″}{e}^{3t}+6{u}^{′}{e}^{3t}+9u{e}^{3t})-6({u}^{′}{e}^{3t}+3u{e}^{3t})+9(u{e}^{3t}) & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}{e}^{3t}+6{u}^{′}{e}^{3t}+9u{e}^{3t}-6{u}^{′}{e}^{3t}-18u{e}^{3t}+9u{e}^{3t} & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}{e}^{3t} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{3t}\\ne 0$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n{w}^{′}=0.\n$$\n\nHence, the solution is\n\n$$\nw={c}_{1}.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have:\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}={c}_{1}\\,⟹\\,u={c}_{1}t+{c}_{2}\n$$\n\nThus, our expression for ${y}_{2}(t)=u{e}^{3t}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(t)={e}^{3t}({c}_{1}t+{c}_{2}).\n$$\n\nSetting ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(t)={e}^{3t}\\cdot t=t{e}^{3t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})={e}^{6t}\\ne 0,$ we know that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{3t}+Bt{e}^{3t}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348313 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "434f78ca-52b2-58d1-875b-8ede183b8607" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-4x}+Bx{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345934} {:answer/id #uuid "65e89a6b-3913-58da-974b-041cf28e1c35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346240} {:answer/id #uuid "6d2bf50d-6c6f-5ccf-8d52-faf693ccf4a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{8x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346302} {:answer/id #uuid "a0cc2591-bb5a-54a4-a261-71f4468c041e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-4x}+Bx{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346755} {:answer/id #uuid "f0e415f8-91e1-53cd-95d8-d8e028805fb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-4x}+Bx{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347477}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f0e415f8-91e1-53cd-95d8-d8e028805fb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-4x}+Bx{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347477} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d8430b0-33d7-5e46-bc4e-72b31f1fff93" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347679}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "90de7b9f-c61d-5f75-969e-a38f9dc27a90" :question/math-academy-id "q-79931" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{-4x}$ is a solution to the equation ${y}^{″}+8{y}^{′}+16y=0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(x)$ to the equation, and write down the general solution." :question/worked-solution "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(x){e}^{-4x}.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{e}^{-4x}-4u{e}^{-4x} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{e}^{-4x}-8{u}^{′}{e}^{-4x}+16u{e}^{-4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({u}^{″}{e}^{-4x}-8{u}^{′}{e}^{-4x}+16u{e}^{-4})+8({u}^{′}{e}^{-4x}-4u{e}^{-4x})+16(u{e}^{-4x}) & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}{e}^{-4x}-8{u}^{′}{e}^{-4x}+16u{e}^{-4x}+8{u}^{′}{e}^{-4x}-32u{e}^{-4x}+16u{e}^{-4x} & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}{e}^{-4x} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{-4x}\\ne 0$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating the equation twice gives\n\n$$\nu={c}_{1}x+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nThus, our expression for ${y}_{2}(x)=u{e}^{-4x}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-4x}({c}_{1}x+{c}_{2}).\n$$\n\nSetting ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-4x}\\cdot x=x{e}^{-4x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})={e}^{-8x}\\ne 0,$ we know that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-4x}+Bx{e}^{-4x}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348314 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ddbd5d0-6591-5bed-bd60-b34243a1c45c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{x/2}+B{e}^{x/4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345758} {:answer/id #uuid "6cfead20-d864-51e6-abe6-511ee7361ca6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{x/2}+Bx{e}^{x/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346300} {:answer/id #uuid "bcfb650c-95dc-502c-9290-f52396d70f2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{x/2}+Bx{e}^{x/4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346993} {:answer/id #uuid "d58dbd6d-4f3d-5571-903a-c23ba18b8bb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{x/2}+Bx{e}^{-x/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347229} {:answer/id #uuid "ed57f949-7746-55e1-ba7d-8a17e2cb2e6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{x/2}+B{e}^{-x/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347445}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6cfead20-d864-51e6-abe6-511ee7361ca6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{x/2}+Bx{e}^{x/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346300} :answer-field/id #uuid "98f6bc4f-46d6-5654-bc1a-c2f03e4d6ce7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347875}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "147caf77-f1af-5f28-82fa-ec6a9d091c31" :question/math-academy-id "q-79932" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x/2}$ is a solution to the equation $4{y}^{″}-4{y}^{′}+y=0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(x)$ to the equation, and write down the general solution." :question/worked-solution "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(x){e}^{x/2}.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{e}^{x/2}+\\frac{u}{2}{e}^{x/2} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{e}^{x/2}+{u}^{′}{e}^{x/2}+\\frac{u}{4}{e}^{x/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\left({u}^{″}{e}^{x/2}+{u}^{′}{e}^{x/2}+\\frac{u}{4}{e}^{x/2}\\right)-4\\left({u}^{′}{e}^{x/2}+\\frac{u}{2}{e}^{x/2}\\right)+u{e}^{x/2} & =0 \\\\ 4{u}^{″}{e}^{x/2}+4{u}^{′}{e}^{x/2}+u{e}^{x/2}-4{u}^{′}{e}^{x/2}-2u{e}^{x/2}+u{e}^{x/2} & =0 \\\\ 4{u}^{″}{e}^{x/2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $4{e}^{x/2}\\ne 0$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating the equation twice gives\n\n$$\nu={c}_{1}x+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nThus, our expression for ${y}_{2}(x)=u{e}^{x/2}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{x/2}({c}_{1}x+{c}_{2}).\n$$\n\nSetting ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{x/2}\\cdot x=x{e}^{x/2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})={e}^{x}\\ne 0,$ we know that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{x/2}+Bx{e}^{x/2}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357490 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "320f4b21-2707-5091-a2f6-3f46b3db3db9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+B\\operatorname{sec}⁡3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355103} {:answer/id #uuid "732019b1-4135-57f2-b0d0-0be5c43e75f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+B\\operatorname{tan}⁡3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355678} {:answer/id #uuid "7905340a-4ac8-558d-bb3d-307b21001f47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+B" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355722} {:answer/id #uuid "a886961d-560b-5f03-a7b4-90a1a7508fea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356157} {:answer/id #uuid "e1d7e1eb-54ff-5d68-969e-6e99529e477b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+Bx\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356637}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a886961d-560b-5f03-a7b4-90a1a7508fea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356157} :answer-field/id #uuid "296c90ca-5225-52de-bd8b-f92f0038d08f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356985}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8c4c99c3-e164-55a4-8452-aa0c671a6241" :question/math-academy-id "q-90911" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x$ is a solution to the equation ${y}^{″}+9y=0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(x)$ to the equation, and write down the general solution." :question/worked-solution "Work first on an interval where the given solution $y_1(x)$ is nonzero. After simplifying $y_2$, verify it in the original equation to extend it across zeros of $y_1$ when the simplified function is defined there.\n\nAccording to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-3u\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-6{u}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x-9u\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({u}^{″}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-6{u}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x-9u\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x)+9u\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-6{u}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″} & =6{u}^{′}\\operatorname{tan}⁡3x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n{w}^{′}=6w\\operatorname{tan}⁡3x.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw={c}_{1}{\\sec }^{2}⁡3x.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}={c}_{1}{\\sec }^{2}⁡3x\\,⟹\\,u=\\frac{{c}_{1}}{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡3x+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(x)=u\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x$ becomes \n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x\\left(\\frac{{c}_{1}}{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡3x+{c}_{2}\\right).\n$$\n\nSetting ${c}_{1}=3$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x(\\operatorname{tan}⁡3x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=3\\ne 0,$ we know that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380076 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cfeae139-47b1-5274-ac73-cf0aeb63739f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{2t}+Bt{e}^{2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380078} {:answer/id #uuid "f053ecae-2167-53a5-8063-9943d9c78817" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{2t}+Bt{e}^{5t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380079} {:answer/id #uuid "a2dc3c9f-5cfd-5654-a8aa-55c2d269de10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{2t}+B{e}^{-t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380080} {:answer/id #uuid "7980db3d-c7f6-51ac-9395-36a543a06764" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{2t}+B{e}^{4t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380081} {:answer/id #uuid "dce1f207-2c54-5b32-b408-b3a423f6c64e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{2t}+B{e}^{3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380082}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cfeae139-47b1-5274-ac73-cf0aeb63739f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{2t}+Bt{e}^{2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380078} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd669795-d3ad-5129-89e4-1459a8c854ec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380077}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c5b1db61-3e18-5223-aa34-4744d437b998" :question/math-academy-id "q-77671" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(t)={e}^{2t}$ is a solution to the equation ${y}^{″}-4{y}^{′}+4y=0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(t)$ to the equation, and write down the general solution." :question/worked-solution "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(t){e}^{2t}.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{e}^{2t}+2u{e}^{2t} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{e}^{2t}+4{u}^{′}{e}^{2t}+4u{e}^{2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({u}^{″}{e}^{2t}+4{u}^{′}{e}^{2t}+4u{e}^{2t})-4({u}^{′}{e}^{2t}+2u{e}^{2t})+4(u{e}^{2t}) & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}{e}^{2t}+4{u}^{′}{e}^{2t}+4u{e}^{2t}-4{u}^{′}{e}^{2t}-8u{e}^{2t}+4u{e}^{2t} & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}{e}^{2t} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{2t}\\ne 0$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating the equation twice gives\n\n$$\nu={c}_{1}t+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nThus, our expression for ${y}_{2}(t)=u{e}^{2t}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(t)={e}^{2t}({c}_{1}t+{c}_{2}).\n$$\n\nSetting ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(t)={e}^{2t}\\cdot t=t{e}^{2t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})={e}^{4t}\\ne 0,$ we know that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{2t}+Bt{e}^{2t}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380277 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "67952861-f148-5199-901d-802ad7eb404f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380279} {:answer/id #uuid "ac8be863-428e-5b87-b241-96a67407c8ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+Bx\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380280} {:answer/id #uuid "107ff186-6c17-5ea7-adea-7b445653f698" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+B\\operatorname{tan}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380281} {:answer/id #uuid "7b6b5b7d-1fb2-5652-86d6-70a0293db99a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+B\\operatorname{sec}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380282} {:answer/id #uuid "a9b43670-c885-5264-801c-5f50ae728f32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+B" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380283}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "67952861-f148-5199-901d-802ad7eb404f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380279} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe6acf30-551e-5b2f-95cc-84791c4dfae6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380278}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e072db4c-ae23-5874-8e05-5e0e80f81647" :question/math-academy-id "q-90912" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is a solution to the equation ${y}^{″}+y=0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(x)$ to the equation, and write down the general solution." :question/worked-solution "Work first on an interval where the given solution $y_1(x)$ is nonzero. After simplifying $y_2$, verify it in the original equation to extend it across zeros of $y_1$ when the simplified function is defined there.\n\nAccording to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-u\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2{u}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-u\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({u}^{″}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2{u}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-u\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+u\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2{u}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″} & =2{u}^{′}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n{w}^{′}=2w\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw={c}_{1}{\\sec }^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}={c}_{1}{\\sec }^{2}⁡x\\,⟹\\,u={c}_{1}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(x)=u\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x({c}_{1}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+{c}_{2}).\n$$\n\nSetting ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=1\\ne 0,$ we know that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421822 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3da93a06-f053-5c86-a2c8-81c4330a3717" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421793} {:answer/id #uuid "6ffd0753-e57e-5d12-924f-8879651936b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421796} {:answer/id #uuid "b0a79f06-5f3d-5f90-b216-f1e59a6b2bd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-3x}+B" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421809} {:answer/id #uuid "cc6c0735-9902-5936-acbf-abb753290129" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421812} {:answer/id #uuid "fdd3af73-a6f5-544b-af96-70b07bb9ad47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421814}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3da93a06-f053-5c86-a2c8-81c4330a3717" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421793} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c6e7e2e-f610-5676-aec5-d60e36c7c4f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421815}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ab6bb605-4b84-5240-9d01-8856c6e45ebb" :question/math-academy-id "q-90696" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{-3x}$ is a solution to the equation ${y}^{″}-9y=0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(x)$ to the equation, and write down the general solution." :question/worked-solution "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(x){e}^{-3x}.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{e}^{-3x}-3u{e}^{-3x} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{e}^{-3x}-6{u}^{′}{e}^{-3x}+9u{e}^{-3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{″}{e}^{-3x}-6{u}^{′}{e}^{-3x}+9u{e}^{-3x}-9u{e}^{-3x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{-3x}({u}^{″}-6{u}^{′}+9u-9u) & =0 \\\\ {e}^{-3x}({u}^{″}-6{u}^{′}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{-3x}\\ne 0$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{″}-6{u}^{′}=0.\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n{w}^{′}-6w=0.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw={c}_{1}{e}^{6x}.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}={c}_{1}{e}^{6x}\\,⟹\\,u=\\frac{1}{6}{c}_{1}{e}^{6x}+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(x)=u{e}^{-3x}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-3x}\\left(\\frac{1}{6}{c}_{1}{e}^{6x}+{c}_{2}\\right).\n$$\n\nSetting ${c}_{1}=6$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-3x}\\cdot {e}^{6x}={e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=6\\ne 0,$ we know that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{3x}.\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding General Solutions to Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs Using Reduction of Order"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152293 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151985 :question/id #uuid "f41bad20-5a72-5d6d-82c4-cf36f7ac74cd" :question/math-academy-id "e-6794" :question/problem "Given that $y_1(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x}$ is a solution to the equation $x^2 y'' + 3xy' + y = 0$ for $x>0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution $y_2(x)$ to the equation, and write down the general solution." :question/worked-solution "According to the reduction of order method, we let \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =u(x){y}_{1}(x) \\\\  & =u(x)\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{u(x)}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $y_{2} = \\dfrac{u(x)}{x}$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2} & =\\frac{u}{x} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{′} & =\\frac{{u}^{′}x-u}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & =\\frac{{u}^{″}{x}^{2}-2{u}^{′}x+2u}{{x}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+3x{y}^{′}+y & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}\\left(\\frac{{u}^{″}{x}^{2}-2{u}^{′}x+2u}{{x}^{3}}\\right)+3x\\left(\\frac{{u}^{′}x-u}{{x}^{2}}\\right)+\\frac{u}{x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{{u}^{″}{x}^{2}-2{u}^{′}x+2u+3{u}^{′}x-3u+u}{x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{{u}^{″}{x}^{2}+{u}^{′}x}{x} & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}x+{u}^{′} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let $w = u'.$ Then our equation becomes a first-order equation:\n\n$$\nw' x + w = 0\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw = \\dfrac{c_1}{x}.\n$$\n\nSince $w = u',$ we have\n\n$$\nu' = \\dfrac{c_1}{x} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad u = c_1\\ln{x} + c_2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for $y_2(x) = \\dfrac{u}{x}$ becomes\n\n$$\ny_2(x) = \\dfrac{c_1\\ln{x} + c_2}{x}.\n$$\n\nSetting $c_1 = 1$ and $c_2 = 0,$ we get\n\n$$\ny_2(x) = \\dfrac{\\ln{x}}{x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W(y_1, y_2) = \\dfrac{1}{x^3} \\neq 0,$ we know that $y_1$ and $y_2$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{A}{x} + \\dfrac{B\\ln{x}}{x} = \\dfrac{A + B\\ln{x}}{x}.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9ef8a6ef-3e77-5e3e-ac75-c3dece0af96e" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152636 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a8b5784a-35ca-524d-aca9-e5e6c58e6c88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = Ax + Bxe^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150324} {:answer/id #uuid "cc70ee60-38d0-575d-b97a-8fd53f3867cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = Ax + Bx^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150325} {:answer/id #uuid "df6d9ebb-9b71-5f9f-b203-c4e1f796971e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = Ax\\ln Bx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150326} {:answer/id #uuid "7548f7c0-83c1-504c-bd00-faf3d805f151" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = Ax + Bx\\ln x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150327} {:answer/id #uuid "7778c1dd-2dd4-5103-aa4c-e633d34e0f28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = Ax^{2}e^{-Bx}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150328}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7548f7c0-83c1-504c-bd00-faf3d805f151" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = Ax + Bx\\ln x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150327} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae926fc8-462c-553a-bf20-287588ec7de4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150323}] :question/id #uuid "bdb26a34-ee4a-5900-98a9-c0d9cd581cd5" :question/math-academy-id "q-65105" :question/problem "Given that $y_{1}(x) = x$ is a solution to the equation $x^{2}y^{″} - xy^{′} + y = 0$ for $x > 0$, use reduction of order to find a second solution $y_{2}(x)$ to the equation, and write down the general solution." :question/worked-solution "Set $y_2=u(x)x$. We have $y_2'=u'x+u$ and $y_2''=u''x+2u'$, so substitution yields $x^2(xu''+u')=0$. Since $x>0$, set $w=u'$ and solve $xw'+w=0$. This gives $w=C/x$ and $u=C\\ln x+D$. Choosing $C=1,D=0$ yields $y_2=x\\ln x$. The Wronskian of $x$ and $x\\ln x$ is $x\\ne0$ on $(0,\\infty)$, so\n$$\ny=Ax+Bx\\ln x.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152642 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "75b181aa-f301-5c7f-b254-cdbb1c399542" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{x}^{3}+B{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379856} {:answer/id #uuid "003bc929-c128-5357-9150-29ca3cf87417" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{x}^{3}+B{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379857} {:answer/id #uuid "fd6960b1-4d6b-5327-bec5-62411056b13e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{x}^{3}+B\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379858} {:answer/id #uuid "2f45a811-b3de-5442-bf6d-e46f435baba6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{x}^{3}+B{x}^{-4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379859} {:answer/id #uuid "564018a2-4319-5125-8266-a3329ca443bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{x}^{3}+B{x}^{-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379860}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "003bc929-c128-5357-9150-29ca3cf87417" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{x}^{3}+B{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379857} :answer-field/id #uuid "e2cfba2c-834b-5adc-946f-1ebe05a93118" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379855}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "d03f0981-7935-5f04-8cb2-c970137dc19f" :question/math-academy-id "q-65121" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{3}$ is a solution to the equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}-5x{y}^{′}+9y=0$ for $x>0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(x)$ to the equation, and write down the general solution." :question/worked-solution "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(x){x}^{3}.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{x}^{3}+3u{x}^{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{x}^{3}+6{u}^{′}{x}^{2}+6ux.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}({u}^{″}{x}^{3}+6{u}^{′}{x}^{2}+6ux)-5x({u}^{′}{x}^{3}+3u{x}^{2})+9(u{x}^{3}) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}({u}^{″}{x}^{2}+6{u}^{′}x+6u-5{u}^{′}x-15u+9u) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}({u}^{″}{x}^{2}+{u}^{′}x) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{4}({u}^{″}x+{u}^{′}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${x}^{4}\\ne 0$ for $x>0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{″}x+{u}^{′}=0.\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n{w}^{′}x+w=0.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw=\\frac{{c}_{1}}{x}.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}=\\frac{{c}_{1}}{x}\\,⟹\\,u={c}_{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(x)=u{x}^{3}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)={x}^{3}({c}_{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{c}_{2}).\n$$\n\nSetting ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)={x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})={x}^{5}\\ne 0,$ we know that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{x}^{3}+B{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265797 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f0eaa94-a5f9-58ca-9a27-30482ef021a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{A}{t}+Bt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263287} {:answer/id #uuid "4cfa65de-78e3-59c7-9186-d46d022f2533" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{A}{t}+B\\sqrt[3]{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263668} {:answer/id #uuid "919d1adf-1695-5680-9426-d9ccf33c85aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{A}{t}+B\\sqrt{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264248} {:answer/id #uuid "bcec159e-74df-5f20-958f-f7dbc9b9d2a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{A}{t}+B\\operatorname{ln}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264639} {:answer/id #uuid "ffbe7899-69fb-56ff-8d7d-17d0c535f498" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{A}{t}+B{t}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265248}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "919d1adf-1695-5680-9426-d9ccf33c85aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{A}{t}+B\\sqrt{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264248} :answer-field/id #uuid "5910bf2d-6191-5e1e-bc28-6ccffa9836a8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265402}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "2b4ddb18-0f3e-54a1-992c-70260602e2de" :question/math-academy-id "q-130012" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(t)=\\frac{1}{t}$ is a solution to the equation $2{t}^{2}{y}^{″}+3t{y}^{′}-y=0$ for $t>0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(t)$ to the equation, and write down the general solution." :question/worked-solution "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(t){t}^{-1}.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{t}^{-1}-u{t}^{-2} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{t}^{-1}-2{u}^{′}{t}^{-2}+2u{t}^{-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{t}^{2}({u}^{″}{t}^{-1}-2{u}^{′}{t}^{-2}+2u{t}^{-3})+3t({u}^{′}{t}^{-1}-u{t}^{-2})-u{t}^{-1} & =0 \\\\ 2{u}^{″}t-4{u}^{′}+4u{t}^{-1}+3{u}^{′}-3u{t}^{-1}-u{t}^{-1} & =0 \\\\ 2{u}^{″}t-{u}^{′} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n2t{w}^{′}-w=0.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw={c}_{1}\\sqrt{t}.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}={c}_{1}\\sqrt{t}\\,⟹\\,u=\\frac{2{c}_{1}}{3}{t}^{3/2}+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(t)=u{t}^{-1}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(t)=\\left(\\frac{2{c}_{1}}{3}{t}^{3/2}+{c}_{2}\\right){t}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nSetting ${c}_{1}=\\frac{3}{2}$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(t)={t}^{3/2}\\cdot {t}^{-1}={t}^{1/2}=\\sqrt{t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}}\\ne 0,$ we know that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{A}{t}+B\\sqrt{t}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265798 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "018e8bd8-204b-52d8-a30a-9f67cdf19eb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{t}^{2}+\\frac{B}{\\sqrt{t}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263013} {:answer/id #uuid "476b9198-e349-5170-9844-db2da6772888" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{t}^{2}+B{t}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263618} {:answer/id #uuid "805d4884-2d82-5979-9794-8dd29d329892" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{t}^{2}+B\\sqrt[3]{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264104} {:answer/id #uuid "e6975bd6-251c-5c4c-b2b5-0e5966849557" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{t}^{2}+\\frac{B}{{t}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265009} {:answer/id #uuid "f98586b7-5873-5a84-98d7-8533f404c86d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{t}^{2}+Bt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265182}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "476b9198-e349-5170-9844-db2da6772888" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{t}^{2}+B{t}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263618} :answer-field/id #uuid "1bdf2b87-3507-598a-9c08-c1ba80a9b502" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265292}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "31386a54-f6f9-57eb-a75f-ecb8a5743f83" :question/math-academy-id "q-130017" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(t)={t}^{2}$ is a solution to the equation ${t}^{2}{y}^{″}-4t{y}^{′}+6y=0$ for $t>0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(t)$ to the equation, and write down the general solution." :question/worked-solution "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(t){t}^{2}.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{t}^{2}+2ut \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{t}^{2}+4{u}^{′}t+2u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}({u}^{″}{t}^{2}+4{u}^{′}t+2u)-4t({u}^{′}{t}^{2}+2ut)+6u{t}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}{t}^{4}+4{u}^{′}{t}^{3}+2u{t}^{2}-4{u}^{′}{t}^{3}-8u{t}^{2}+6u{t}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}{t}^{4} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t>0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n{w}^{′}=0.\n$$\n\nThe solution is\n\n$$\nw={c}_{1}\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}={c}_{1}\\,⟹\\,u={c}_{1}t+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(t)=u{t}^{2}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(t)=({c}_{1}t+{c}_{2}){t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSetting ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(t)=t\\cdot {t}^{2}={t}^{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})={t}^{4}\\ne 0,$ we know that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{t}^{2}+B{t}^{3}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265800 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f5c2990-0c8a-584d-bfde-31963f7ff692" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=At+Bt\\operatorname{ln}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263135} {:answer/id #uuid "784e1679-3718-5a06-a425-4c1bcafd5944" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=At+B{t}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264044} {:answer/id #uuid "9d9700b6-666a-5f29-a8bb-ff7cd542805c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=At+\\frac{B}{{t}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264349} {:answer/id #uuid "f1e28c2f-fe2a-57e6-bebd-5dd0362f05e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=At+\\frac{B}{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265126} {:answer/id #uuid "ffcf23b6-c4a2-5b74-ab79-b90a02e66db9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=At+B{e}^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265250}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d9700b6-666a-5f29-a8bb-ff7cd542805c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=At+\\frac{B}{{t}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264349} :answer-field/id #uuid "b667bc8d-3635-50e2-acc5-7b154ab5806c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265565}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "fa1dae64-83b4-59f1-8ce4-3c9cac8a6ed9" :question/math-academy-id "q-130028" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(t)=t$ is a solution to the equation ${t}^{2}{y}^{″}+2t{y}^{′}-2y=0$ for $t>0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(t)$ to the equation, and write down the general solution." :question/worked-solution "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(t)t.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}t+u \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}t+2{u}^{′}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}({u}^{″}t+2{u}^{′})+2t({u}^{′}t+u)-2ut & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}{t}^{3}+2{u}^{′}{t}^{2}+2{u}^{′}{t}^{2}+2ut-2ut & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}{t}^{3}+4{u}^{′}{t}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {t}^{2}({u}^{″}t+4{u}^{′}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t>0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{″}t+4{u}^{′}=0.\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n{w}^{′}t+4w=0.\n$$\n\nThe solution is\n\n$$\nw=\\frac{{c}_{1}}{{t}^{4}}.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}=\\frac{{c}_{1}}{{t}^{4}}\\,⟹\\,u=-\\frac{{c}_{1}}{3{t}^{3}}+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(t)=ut$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(t)=\\left(-\\frac{{c}_{1}}{3{t}^{3}}+{c}_{2}\\right)t.\n$$\n\nSetting ${c}_{1}=-3$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(t)=\\frac{1}{{t}^{3}}\\cdot t=\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=-\\frac{3}{{t}^{2}}\\ne 0,$ we know that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=At+\\frac{B}{{t}^{2}}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265806 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e01c9a7-148d-5bd3-89a8-a82a160e377b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{x}^{3}+B{x}^{7/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263387} {:answer/id #uuid "53994796-807c-5699-809a-4fdc2f13efec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{x}^{3}+\\frac{B}{{x}^{5/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263722} {:answer/id #uuid "c2acce5d-ff33-5505-b710-ccba19ef90b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{x}^{3}+\\frac{B}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264694} {:answer/id #uuid "cfadfc2b-a079-5fcf-91df-aa65bd5c90ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{x}^{3}+B{x}^{4/3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264819} {:answer/id #uuid "ed5b985d-2cdf-53ec-8f5f-c74d0a945f4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{x}^{3}+B{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265074}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "53994796-807c-5699-809a-4fdc2f13efec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{x}^{3}+\\frac{B}{{x}^{5/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263722} :answer-field/id #uuid "83369fcf-1351-5c90-8bff-62b0f6867940" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265477}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "ce8f178b-217a-592c-86ce-c1cebc15d615" :question/math-academy-id "q-130040" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{3}$ is a solution to the equation $2{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+x{y}^{′}-15y=0$ for $x>0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(x)$ to the equation, and write down the general solution." :question/worked-solution "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(x){x}^{3}.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{x}^{3}+3u{x}^{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{x}^{3}+6{u}^{′}{x}^{2}+6ux.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}({u}^{″}{x}^{3}+6{u}^{′}{x}^{2}+6ux)+x({u}^{′}{x}^{3}+3u{x}^{2})-15u{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ 2{u}^{″}{x}^{5}+12{u}^{′}{x}^{4}+12u{x}^{3}+{u}^{′}{x}^{4}+3u{x}^{3}-15u{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ 2{u}^{″}{x}^{5}+13{u}^{′}{x}^{4} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{4}(2{u}^{″}x+13{u}^{′}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $x>0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n2{u}^{″}x+13{u}^{′}=0.\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n2{w}^{′}x+13w=0.\n$$\n\nThe solution is\n\n$$\nw={c}_{1}{x}^{-13/2}.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}={c}_{1}{x}^{-13/2}\\,⟹\\,u=-\\frac{2{c}_{1}}{11}{x}^{-11/2}+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(x)=u{x}^{3}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=\\left(-\\frac{2{c}_{1}}{11}{x}^{-11/2}+{c}_{2}\\right){x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nSetting ${c}_{1}=-\\frac{11}{2}$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)={x}^{-11/2}\\cdot {x}^{3}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{5/2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=-\\frac{11}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\ne 0,$ we know that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{x}^{3}+\\frac{B}{{x}^{5/2}}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335798 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05a97663-0608-51f0-8f43-6ffcee3c9cb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{t}^{3}+B{t}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333038} {:answer/id #uuid "a0197158-d6d7-52a8-9f42-d42b2c9afe9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{t}^{3}+\\frac{B}{{t}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334396} {:answer/id #uuid "a5d1ec01-be58-532f-96d2-67577d1448ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{t}^{3}+\\frac{B}{{t}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334443} {:answer/id #uuid "a8d78850-6d82-5fb4-bb7d-a80419d2f3b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{t}^{3}+Bt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334469} {:answer/id #uuid "b861ef08-ef14-563a-8428-30510f90b503" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{t}^{3}+B{t}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334606}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "05a97663-0608-51f0-8f43-6ffcee3c9cb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{t}^{3}+B{t}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333038} :answer-field/id #uuid "1cc638dc-0d26-5d7d-82d3-0f969658dccc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335292}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "41fc81ca-8d1b-59cf-a5cf-a99524cfb86b" :question/math-academy-id "q-65123" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(t)={t}^{3}$ is a solution to the equation ${t}^{2}{y}^{″}-6t{y}^{′}+12y=0$ for $t>0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(t)$ to the equation, and write down the general solution." :question/worked-solution "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(t){t}^{3}.$\n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{t}^{3}+3u{t}^{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{t}^{3}+6{u}^{′}{t}^{2}+6ut.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}({u}^{″}{t}^{3}+6{u}^{′}{t}^{2}+6ut)-6t({u}^{′}{t}^{3}+3u{t}^{2})+12(u{t}^{3}) & =0 \\\\ {t}^{3}({u}^{″}{t}^{2}+6{u}^{′}t+6u-6{u}^{′}t-18u+12u) & =0 \\\\ {t}^{3}({u}^{″}{t}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ {t}^{5}({u}^{″}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${t}^{5}\\ne 0$ for $t>0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n{w}^{′}=0.\n$$\n\nThe solution is\n\n$$\nw={c}_{1}.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}={c}_{1}\\,⟹\\,u={c}_{1}t+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(t)=u{t}^{3}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(t)={t}^{3}({c}_{1}t+{c}_{2}).\n$$\n\nSetting ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(t)=t\\cdot {t}^{3}={t}^{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})={t}^{6}\\ne 0,$ we know that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{t}^{3}+B{t}^{4}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348315 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1618a4ce-9256-597e-9e33-150f1a8a187a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{t}^{2}+B\\frac{1}{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345544} {:answer/id #uuid "3e87cd26-ef37-5f15-9466-1fea306ebedc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{t}^{2}+B{t}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345882} {:answer/id #uuid "724fd0c5-a371-5eed-92c8-17a7a441fca4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{t}^{2}+Bt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346363} {:answer/id #uuid "ce28a91c-ffed-5f67-9251-1f1951696ad9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{t}^{2}+B{t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347155} {:answer/id #uuid "eaf0f9cb-0def-51e9-a83d-b6f2a649b502" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{t}^{2}+B{t}^{-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347424}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "724fd0c5-a371-5eed-92c8-17a7a441fca4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{t}^{2}+Bt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346363} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec01f2a5-d1aa-5251-bb23-3b87a05cf681" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348024}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "4a520f91-bb99-5d2e-8e5a-db7cbd19c2f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-79934" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(t)={t}^{2}$ is a solution to the equation ${t}^{2}{y}^{″}-2t{y}^{′}+2y=0$ for $t>0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(t)$ to the equation, and write down the general solution." :question/worked-solution "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(t){t}^{2}.$\n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{t}^{2}+2ut \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{t}^{2}+4{u}^{′}t+2u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}({u}^{″}{t}^{2}+4{u}^{′}t+2u)-2t({u}^{′}{t}^{2}+2ut)+2(u{t}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}{t}^{4}+4{u}^{′}{t}^{3}+2u{t}^{2}-2{u}^{′}{t}^{3}-4u{t}^{2}+2u{t}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}{t}^{4}+2{u}^{′}{t}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {t}^{3}(t{u}^{″}+2{u}^{′}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${t}^{3}\\ne 0$ for $t>0,$ we must have\n\n$$\nt{u}^{″}+2{u}^{′}=0.\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\nt{w}^{′}+2w=0.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw=\\frac{{c}_{1}}{{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}=\\frac{{c}_{1}}{{t}^{2}}\\,⟹\\,u=-\\frac{{c}_{1}}{t}+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(t)=u{t}^{2}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(t)=\\left(-\\frac{{c}_{1}}{t}+{c}_{2}\\right){t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSetting ${c}_{1}=-1$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(t)=t.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=-{t}^{2}\\ne 0,$ we know that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{t}^{2}+Bt.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348316 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f02fb94-4849-5cea-b750-8c7a10c9a50e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{x}^{2}+B{x}^{1/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345476} {:answer/id #uuid "300ee0a3-7a1c-517d-91c6-279210337c37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{x}^{2}+B{x}^{3/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345778} {:answer/id #uuid "3f9d59a4-626f-5c4d-9546-3fe500a1aace" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{x}^{2}+B{x}^{-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345891} {:answer/id #uuid "93d2aed1-5f5c-5120-8251-a6fd4ba4d823" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{x}^{2}+B{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346641} {:answer/id #uuid "94ffb949-1659-58a7-af6b-6fc0f2db6805" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{x}^{2}+B{x}^{-3/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346652}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0f02fb94-4849-5cea-b750-8c7a10c9a50e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{x}^{2}+B{x}^{1/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345476} :answer-field/id #uuid "717cbfeb-8c94-51dc-97dd-4c8b880447ac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347807}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "811046b6-f1ce-59a7-8283-c160f30a4a74" :question/math-academy-id "q-79935" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{2}$ is a solution to the equation $2{x}^{2}{y}^{″}-3x{y}^{′}+2y=0$ for $x>0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(x)$ to the equation, and write down the general solution." :question/worked-solution "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(x){x}^{2}.$\n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{x}^{2}+2ux \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{x}^{2}+4{u}^{′}x+2u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}({u}^{″}{x}^{2}+4{u}^{′}x+2u)-3x({u}^{′}{x}^{2}+2ux)+2(u{x}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ 2{u}^{″}{x}^{4}+8{u}^{′}{x}^{3}+4u{x}^{2}-3{u}^{′}{x}^{3}-6u{x}^{2}+2u{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 2{u}^{″}{x}^{4}+5{u}^{′}{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}(2x{u}^{″}+5{u}^{′}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${x}^{3}\\ne 0$ for $x>0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n2x{u}^{″}+5{u}^{′}=0.\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n2x{w}^{′}+5w=0.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw={c}_{1}{x}^{-5/2}.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}={c}_{1}{x}^{-5/2}\\,⟹\\,u=-\\frac{2{c}_{1}}{3}{x}^{-3/2}+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, our expression for ${y}_{2}(x)=u{x}^{2}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=\\left(-\\frac{2{c}_{1}}{3}{x}^{-3/2}+{c}_{2}\\right){x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSetting ${c}_{1}=-\\frac{3}{2}$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=({x}^{-3/2}){x}^{2}={x}^{1/2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=-\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{3/2}\\ne 0,$ we know that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{x}^{2}+B{x}^{1/2}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357476 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bf2cf10-889a-5e2c-a4c9-c7d6aadbce53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{x}^{3}+\\frac{B}{{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354772} {:answer/id #uuid "aa16421b-994d-5da6-863d-6ea3a79896bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{x}^{3}+\\frac{B}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356170} {:answer/id #uuid "c14821fb-4141-5115-9012-5b4f4293ea36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{x}^{3}+B{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356356} {:answer/id #uuid "e9843801-abf9-5cf7-a76d-830f177eef7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{x}^{3}+Bx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356706} {:answer/id #uuid "eba3f05e-2836-5879-ae2f-d7c77543ae46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{x}^{3}+B{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356721}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aa16421b-994d-5da6-863d-6ea3a79896bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{x}^{3}+\\frac{B}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356170} :answer-field/id #uuid "02d6a845-e776-58ed-a2a7-d5b5b0fd2a6c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356915}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "25f23664-d443-5442-98c5-f5ec1e9dae76" :question/math-academy-id "q-90763" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{3}$ is a solution to the equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}-6y=0$ for $x>0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(x)$ to the equation, and write down the general solution." :question/worked-solution "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(x){x}^{3}.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{x}^{3}+3u{x}^{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{x}^{3}+6{u}^{′}{x}^{2}+6ux.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}({u}^{″}{x}^{3}+6{u}^{′}{x}^{2}+6ux)-6(u{x}^{3}) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}({u}^{″}{x}^{2}+6{u}^{′}x+6u-6u) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}({u}^{″}{x}^{2}+6{u}^{′}x) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{4}({u}^{″}x+6{u}^{′}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${x}^{4}\\ne 0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{″}x+6{u}^{′}=0.\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\nx{w}^{′}+6w=0.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw=\\frac{{c}_{1}}{{x}^{6}}.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}=\\frac{{c}_{1}}{{x}^{6}}\\,⟹\\,u=-\\frac{{c}_{1}}{5{x}^{5}}+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(x)=u{x}^{3}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)={x}^{3}\\left(-\\frac{{c}_{1}}{5{x}^{5}}+{c}_{2}\\right).\n$$\n\nSetting ${c}_{1}=-5$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=-5\\ne 0,$ we know that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{x}^{3}+\\frac{B}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421819 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05fa078e-be12-5c3b-bea0-316dbe5fdadb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{x}^{-2}+B{x}^{-1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421787} {:answer/id #uuid "07ce657b-e482-5f99-bc7d-7195f7a26f4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{x}^{-2}+B{x}^{-2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421788} {:answer/id #uuid "4ebf1486-5cc5-5f08-9b5e-e0e154643134" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{x}^{-2}+Bx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421795} {:answer/id #uuid "9bfec298-1d17-523e-a8e3-6399a0f68344" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{x}^{-2}+B{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421806} {:answer/id #uuid "a873b607-3f0b-5c6e-ba8c-a65ec6d7eec3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{x}^{-2}+Bx\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421808}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "07ce657b-e482-5f99-bc7d-7195f7a26f4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{x}^{-2}+B{x}^{-2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421788} :answer-field/id #uuid "564f83a4-e64a-5a32-8a58-5a6072793073" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421816}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "4d0ba009-0a0e-534d-98fc-35f0419611d1" :question/math-academy-id "q-79933" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{-2}$ is a solution to the equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}+5x{y}^{′}+4y=0$ for $x>0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(x)$ to the equation, and write down the general solution." :question/worked-solution "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(x){x}^{-2}.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{x}^{-2}-2u{x}^{-3} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{x}^{-2}-4{u}^{′}{x}^{-3}+6u{x}^{-4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}({u}^{″}{x}^{-2}-4{u}^{′}{x}^{-3}+6u{x}^{-4})+5x({u}^{′}{x}^{-2}-2u{x}^{-3})+4u{x}^{-2} & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}-4{u}^{′}{x}^{-1}+6u{x}^{-2}+5{u}^{′}{x}^{-1}-10u{x}^{-2}+4u{x}^{-2} & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}+{u}^{′}{x}^{-1} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $x\\ne 0$ for $x>0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{″}x+{u}^{′}=0.\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n{w}^{′}x+w=0.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw=\\frac{{c}_{1}}{x}.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}=\\frac{{c}_{1}}{x}\\,⟹\\,u={c}_{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(x)=u{x}^{-2}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)={x}^{-2}({c}_{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{c}_{2}).\n$$\n\nSetting ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)={x}^{-2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})={x}^{-5}\\ne 0,$ we know that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{x}^{-2}+B{x}^{-2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding General Solutions to Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs With Variable Coefficients"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152294 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151912 :question/id #uuid "58f2bf06-7449-5a66-9662-532ce70a8ff9" :question/math-academy-id "e-21879" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation below, for which the corresponding complementary solution is $y_c(x)=Ax^6+Bx^2,$ where $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants.\n\n$$\nx^2y'' - 7xy' + 12y = -3x^3\n$$\n\nDetermine whether $y_p(x)=x$ or $y_p(x)=x^3$ is a particular solution of the differential equation, and then write the general solution." :question/worked-solution "For an inhomogeneous differential equation, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x) = y_c(x) + y_p(x),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $y_c(x)$ is the complementary solution (the general solution of the corresponding homogeneous equation), and\n\n- $y_p(x)$ is a particular solution of the original inhomogeneous equation.\n\nWith this in mind, let's check which of the two given functions is a particular solution of our inhomogeneous equation:\n\n- For $y_p(x)=x,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =(x)^{′}=1, \\\\ {y}^{″}(x) & =(1)^{′}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation, we have\n\n$$\nx^2(0) - 7x(1) + 12(x) = -3x^3. \\:\\: {\\color{red}\\times}\n$$\n\nSo, $y_p(x)=x$ isn't a particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n- For $y_p(x)=x^3,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{3})^{′}=3{x}^{2}, \\\\ {y}^{″}(x) & =(3{x}^{2})^{′}=6x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation, we have\n\n$$\nx^2(6x) - 7x(3x^2) + 12(x^3) = -3x^3. \\:\\: {\\color{darkgreen}\\checkmark}\n$$\n\nSo, $y_p(x)=x^3$ is a particular solution of the differential equation.\n\nWe are told that the complementary solution of our equation is $y_c(x)=Ax^6+Bx^2.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =A{x}^{6}+B{x}^{2}+{x}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7640be27-8277-5a9c-bf14-83bcc7a38604" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152577 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3594a392-68f2-5a5a-ad28-aa038b74bd73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149943} {:answer/id #uuid "91a0180e-f4e9-5fbb-991e-1010a5619806" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/i