bf3de5a07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{-5x}+B{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421811}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "243e57c9-66d3-57a3-8eda-0a620df3bf86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{5x}+B{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421791} :answer-field/id #uuid "41a0da28-1640-525e-93bc-52f7cb608d46" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149955}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "181a7898-7274-5974-9634-f1677406f23f" :question/math-academy-id "q-339880" :question/problem "$$\n{y}^{″}-2{y}^{′}-15y=-7{e}^{4x}\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation above, for which the corresponding complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{5x}+B{e}^{-3x},$ where $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants. Select the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$\nThe function ${y}_{p}(x)={e}^{4x}$ \n{{field-1}} \na particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$\nThe function ${y}_{p}(x)=x{e}^{4x}$ \n{{field-2}} \na particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$\nThe general solution of the differential equation is given by\n\n$\\,\\,$\n$y(x)=$ \n{{field-3}}\n$+$\n{{field-4}},\n\n$\\,$\nwhere \n{{field-5}} are arbitrary constants." :question/worked-solution "For an inhomogeneous differential equation, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}_{c}(x)$ is the complementary solution (the general solution of the corresponding homogeneous equation), and\n\n- ${y}_{p}(x)$ is a particular solution of the original inhomogeneous equation.\n\nWith this in mind, let's check which of the two given functions is a particular solution of our inhomogeneous equation:\n\n- For ${y}_{p}(x)={e}^{4x},$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =({e}^{4x})^{′}=4{e}^{4x}, \\\\ {y}^{″}(x) & =(4{e}^{4x})^{′}=16{e}^{4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation, we have\n\n$$\n(16{e}^{4x})-2(4{e}^{4x})-15({e}^{4x})=-7{e}^{4x}.\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{p}(x)={e}^{4x}$ $\\text{is}$ a particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n- For ${y}_{p}(x)=x{e}^{4x},$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =(x{e}^{4x})^{′}={e}^{4x}+4x{e}^{4x}, \\\\ {y}^{″}(x) & =({e}^{4x}+4x{e}^{4x})^{′}=8{e}^{4x}+16x{e}^{4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation, we have\n\n$$\n(8{e}^{4x}+16x{e}^{4x})-2({e}^{4x}+4x{e}^{4x})-15(x{e}^{4x})=-7{e}^{4x}.\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{p}(x)=x{e}^{4x}$ $\\text{isn't}$ a particular solution of the differential equation.\n\nWe are told that the complementary solution of our equation is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{5x}+B{e}^{-3x}.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =A{e}^{5x}+B{e}^{-3x}+{e}^{4x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A\\text{ and }B$ are arbitrary constants."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152578 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17b21dce-de7a-55b2-8682-5b0efa4bdadf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Cx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312142} {:answer/id #uuid "4de0327b-7e94-56cb-8a19-ff87e144a27c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "C{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312608} {:answer/id #uuid "8cb5dadd-9992-5965-9377-6fd1fb55b637" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313100} {:answer/id #uuid "d71b5c9e-f2f2-527d-a572-56e0e78b85c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313671}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8cb5dadd-9992-5965-9377-6fd1fb55b637" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/v