value "is" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379544} :answer-field/id #uuid "97344f07-9c8b-5a0e-84ff-3f96c4234cff" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379542} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0dd8d274-7905-50f3-90e7-7f22323f4562" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "isn't" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379546} {:answer/id #uuid "82ad698e-734b-595a-9bca-be1e493cc038" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "is" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379547}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0dd8d274-7905-50f3-90e7-7f22323f4562" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "isn't" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379546} :answer-field/id #uuid "45fc80bc-4982-5cb2-b9dc-375ad0dcb1aa" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379545}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "505741bc-4876-51ce-9f83-381bbfa646f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-339875" :question/problem "$$\n{y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}-4y=5{e}^{x}\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation above, for which the corresponding complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{-4x},$ where $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants. Select the correct options below.$\\,$\nThe function ${y}_{p}(x)={e}^{x}$ \n{{field-1}} \na particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$\nThe function ${y}_{p}(x)=x{e}^{x}$ \n{{field-2}} \na particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$\nThe general solution of the differential equation is given by\n\n$\\,\\,$\n$y(x)=$ \n{{field-3}}\n$+$\n{{field-4}},\n\n$\\,$\nwhere \n{{field-5}} are arbitrary constants." :question/worked-solution "For an inhomogeneous differential equation, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}_{c}(x)$ is the complementary solution (the general solution of the corresponding homogeneous equation), and\n\n- ${y}_{p}(x)$ is a particular solution of the original inhomogeneous equation.\n\nWith this in mind, let's check which of the two given functions is a particular solution of our inhomogeneous equation:\n\n- For ${y}_{p}(x)={e}^{x},$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =({e}^{x})^{′}={e}^{x}, \\\\ {y}^{″}(x) & =({e}^{x})^{′}={e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation, we have\n\n$$\n({e}^{x})+3({e}^{x})-4({e}^{x})=5{e}^{x}.\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{p}(x)={e}^{x}$ $\\text{isn't}$ a particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n- For ${y}_{p}(x)=x{e}^{x},$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =(x{e}^{x})^{′}={e}^{x}+x{e}^{x}, \\\\ {y}^{″}(x) & =({e}^{x}+x{e}^{x})^{′}=2{e}^{x}+x{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation, we have\n\n$$\n(2{e}^{x}+x{e}^{x})+3({e}^{x}+x{e}^{x})-4(x{e}^{x})=5{e}^{x}.\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{p}(x)=x{e}^{x}$ $\\text{is}$ a particular solution of the differential equation.\n\nWe are told that the complementary solution of our equation is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{-4x}.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{-4x}+x{e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A\\text{ and }B$ are arbitrary constants."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379548 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9e693f8b-c04f-5460-8cbe-7e1230216dc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "C{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379550} {:answer/id #uuid "ac611c09-6323-5081-a5a9-4ad367023bd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379551} {:answer/id #uuid "ae790c43-b218-54a4-b207-d11d68ed3f2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/