4d5c8c21-2e2d-5495-848e-2b4042a1615c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "isn't" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379566} :answer-field/id #uuid "df580461-f2b5-57e6-9552-7caad82cf0d2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379565}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f71ec5a9-eedc-5e6e-8768-9c975c1b2e00" :question/math-academy-id "q-339915" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}-6x{y}^{′}+10y=-2{x}^{3}\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation above, for which the corresponding complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{x}^{5}+B{x}^{2},$ where $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants. Select the correct options below.$\\,$\nThe function ${y}_{p}(x)={x}^{2}$ \n{{field-1}} \na particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$\nThe function ${y}_{p}(x)={x}^{3}$ \n{{field-2}} \na particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$\nThe general solution of the differential equation is given by\n\n$\\,\\,$\n$y(x)=$ \n{{field-3}}\n$+$\n{{field-4}},\n\n$\\,$\nwhere \n{{field-5}} are arbitrary constants." :question/worked-solution "For an inhomogeneous differential equation, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}_{c}(x)$ is the complementary solution (the general solution of the corresponding homogeneous equation), and\n\n- ${y}_{p}(x)$ is a particular solution of the original inhomogeneous equation.\n\nWith this in mind, let's check which of the two given functions is a particular solution of our inhomogeneous equation:\n\n- For ${y}_{p}(x)={x}^{2},$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{2})^{′}=2x, \\\\ {y}^{″}(x) & =(2x)^{′}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}(2)-6x(2x)+10({x}^{2})=-2{x}^{3}.\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{p}(x)={x}^{2}$ $\\text{isn't}$ a particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n- For ${y}_{p}(x)={x}^{3},$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{3})^{′}=3{x}^{2}, \\\\ {y}^{″}(x) & =(3{x}^{2})^{′}=6x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}(6x)-6x(3{x}^{2})+10({x}^{3})=-2{x}^{3}.\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{p}(x)={x}^{3}$ $\\text{is}$ a particular solution of the differential equation.\n\nWe are told that the complementary solution of our equation is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{x}^{5}+B{x}^{2}.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =A{x}^{5}+B{x}^{2}+{x}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A\\text{ and }B$ are arbitrary constants."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397359 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "de93b087-2c87-54a2-8ce4-861cedbe5c5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "isn't" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397361} {:answer/id #uuid "38b22569-4620-5db6-9463-291c3a777e54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "is" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397362}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "38b22569-4620-5db6-9463-291c3a777e54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "is" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397362} :answer-field/id #uuid "bcb669d8-5345-5c4e-b897-7495dd431c78" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397360} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52998750-f1d9-5a19-9002-6bfcf31c55e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{x}^{4}+B{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397364} {:answer/id #uuid "e45559f1-ba00-51cc-b081-a77f686eee2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{x}^{3}+B" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397365} {:answer/id #uuid "5002b657-e4ab-5c5f